==== Readme file for the DOS and Windows Protected Mode companion
==== disks. Version 1.0   January 1, 1993

This file contains a list of the files included on the companion disk.
All of these files are in a single .ZIP archive, LISTINGS.ZIP.
Each chapter is in its own directory. Chapter 5, for example is
in directory \CHAP5.

To extract the entire set of listings, create a directory, make it
the working directory and run PKUNZIP with the -d option. For example,
if the diskette is in drive A and you want to place the listings in
C:\DWPM you could use the following commands:

C> mkdir \DWPM
C> cd \DWPM
C> pkunzip -d a:\listings.zip

Now, the Chapter 1 listings are in C:\DWPM\CHAP1 and the Chapter
11 listings are in C:\DWPM\CHAP11.

If you only want certain listings, you can run PKUNZIP to extract the
ones you want. For example, if you want to get the DPMI examples
from Chapter 5 to the current directory, and the diskette is in drive
A, you could type the following:

C> pkunzip a:\listings.zip chap5\dpmi*.c

Note that you won't use the -d option in this case since you don't want
PKUNZIP to create any directories.


==== IN CASE OF DIFFICULTY ====
If you have trouble compiling a particular listing check the
following items:

1) Are you using the right compiler (and extender, if applicable)?
   Each main program contains a note about which compilers it
   will work with.

2) Are you using the proper compile directives?
   Each main program contains instructions for its compiling -- some
   programs compile via makefiles -- you should use them where supplied.
   Most of the Windows examples compile with the BWINCOMP (Borland) or
   MWINCOMP (Microsoft) batch files.

3) Is your compiler/extender installed properly? Can you compile
   a simple program, e.g.:
       main() { printf("Hello\n"); }
   If you can't get that to run, you probably need a directory in
   your PATH variable, an environment variable set, or something
   else specific to your compiler or extender.

4) As new versions of extenders and compilers appear, sometimes
   the compile directives required for a program will change. For
   example, Older versions of Phar Lap's 286 extender require the
   LLIBPE.LIB library for int86() and other functions under Microsoft
   C. Newer versions of the extender don't require this library.

5) Phar Lap 286|DOS Extender 2.5 (and Lite) don't fully support
   DLLs with Microsoft C 7.0. Therefore, the examples that create
   DLLs (XCI and TURTLE) don't provide compile instructions for
   C 7.0 -- the DLLs won't work anyway. Use Borland C or Microsoft
   C 6.0 instead.

If you have trouble running a program, check the following items:

1) Do you have enough memory and the right processor for the program?

2) Is your environment compatible? If you can't get the "Hello" program
   in item 3 (above) to  run then you probably have an environment
   conflict.

3) Does your extender require a special syntax to run programs? For
   example, the Rational extender requires you to run T.EXP like this:
       DOS4G T
   Many other extenders have this same requirement.

4) Are you trying to run a program under 286|DOS Extender Lite that
   requires the full 286|DOS Extender package? The INT286, CAT286B2,
   XCI, and TURTLE programs will not work with the Lite extender.


Good luck, and have fun!
Al Williams



